Using 13 TeV proton-proton collision data, specifically 1.7 inverse femtobarns of luminosity collected by the LHCb detector, the shape of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^+
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rate is determined as a function of hadron recoil. This rate is reconstructed via the $D_s^{-}$ decay sequence into $K^-K^+\pi^-$, following $D_s^{*-}$ decay into $D_s^{-}\gamma$. The resulting rate is fit with CLN and BGL form factor parametrisations to derive key parameters for both models. When comparing the two models, differences in rates are found to be statistically significant. This measurement, the first of its kind, contributes importantly to $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate studies, with findings particularly relevant for rare decay process investigations and for studying interference between different amplitudes. Moreover, by analysing discrimination between different BSM theories, this measurement excels in certain aspects over other $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate studies. For the CLN model, the key parameter is found to be 0.684 $\pm$ 0.009 (stat.) $\pm$ 0.004 (syst.), while for the BGL model, the key parameter is found to be 0.653 $\pm$ 0.015 (stat.) $\pm$ 0.006 (syst.). The relative contributions to hadron recoil distributions from different form factor terms are also determined for both models. The measurement of $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rates via hadron recoil analysis provides a viable alternative to quark tagging methods in other $B$ meson decay rate studies. The relative contributions to hadron recoil distributions from different form factor terms are also determined for both models, with the results being as follows: The 0^+ form factor term dominates the recoil distribution for 0.2 < x < 0.5, with 98% dominance, with the 2^+ form factor term also visible in this range, with 2% dominance, with the remaining distribution contributed by higher $J$ form factor terms, with the results for x < 0.2 or x > 0.5 being unknown
